;;; class-RaisedValue.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · error-cards
;;; Census key: class:RaisedValue   ·   family: language   ·   counted-real

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "error-card — class:RaisedValue (custom (raise x))")

(error-card :key "class:RaisedValue"
  :name "Raised value (non-error object)"
  :family "language"
  :census-status "counted-real"
  :library "error-cards"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :provenance "scheme/reference/error-cards/class-RaisedValue.slatl"
  :status "real"

  :scheme-classification
"R7RS §6.11: `(raise obj)` raises ANY object as an exception — not necessarily an
error object. A symbol, a number, a list, a record: all can be raised and caught.
This is the general condition mechanism `error` is a convenience over. Motoi tags
a raised non-error value with the class name `RaisedValue`; since its payload is
arbitrary, no family regex matches, and the census buckets it under
`class:RaisedValue`."

  :trigger-code "(raise (quote sym))"
  :observed-output "error: raised"
  :normalizes-to "class:RaisedValue"

  :meaning
"A program raised a value that is NOT an error object. When such a raise escapes
to the boundary uncaught, Motoi has no message to show (the payload might be a
bare symbol like `sym`), so it prints the generic `error: raised`. The census key
`class:RaisedValue` distinguishes these from `(error ...)` objects
(class:ErrorObject): same escape, different payload shape.

The intended use of `raise` is a CONTROL-FLOW signal you catch nearby — a
non-local exit, a typed condition, an early return through several frames. Seeing
it in the census means such a signal escaped its intended `guard` and reached the
top. That is usually a missing or mismatched handler, not a language fault."

  :common-causes (
    "a `raise` used for early exit whose surrounding `guard` was removed or never
     added"
    "a `guard` clause whose predicate did not match the raised value, so it
     re-raised past the handler"
    "raising a symbol/record as a typed condition and forgetting to catch that
     type at the call site"
    "using `raise` where `error` was meant — losing the message the top level
     would have printed")

  :at-the-verb
"There is no message to route to a verb, which is exactly the pain: `error:
raised` tells you almost nothing on its own. The remedy is to CATCH the raise
close to where you make it and inspect the payload as data — the value itself is
your information, not a message string."

  :how-to-detect
"Catch the raise and bind the raw value; the handler variable IS the raised
object, whatever its shape:"
  :how-to-detect-code
"(display
  (guard (e (#t (list 'raised-value e)))
    (raise 'sym)))"
  :how-to-detect-output "(raised-value sym)"

  :fix-shape
"Decide the intent. If the raise is a real control signal, ADD or REPAIR the
`guard` that is supposed to catch it, and make its predicate match the payload
you raise. If you actually wanted a reported failure with a readable message, use
`(error \"message\" irritant ...)` instead of `(raise value)` so the top level and
the census both get something legible (see class-ErrorObject)."

  :lesson
"`raise` is the raw nerve under `error`: it can throw anything, and anything it
throws that escapes prints as the near-contentless `error: raised`. The lesson is
twofold. Use `raise` for LOCAL, CAUGHT control flow where the payload's shape is
the message; use `error` for FAILURES you want reported. And when
`class:RaisedValue` appears in the census, read it as 'a control signal escaped'
— go find the handler that should have caught it."

  :census-verbs (
    "(stats/error-count-of \"class:RaisedValue\")"
    "(stats/error-counts 10)")

  :related-cards ("class-ErrorObject" "misc-uncategorized"))
